This folder contains unique test configurations used in the SE performance tests.

Each test configuration is known by it's unique file name. It is very important that
this filename not change once it has been used, as this filename gets saved with the
performance results and will be used in queries to filter and return particular
performance data.

TPC-H tests have load commands that have hard coded paths for a given machine. So a
given TPC-H test file may not work in another environment. So we need to encode this
into the filename so it is clearer and differentiated.

TPC-H tests have filenames that are encoded as follows:
  tpch-<scale_factor>-<load_format>.jmx

where <load_format> is a number followed by 'D', for the number of disk drives in the
machine, then another number followed by 'F', for the number of ways the data is
partitioned on each disk.

Examples:
(1) tpch-sf100-2D1F.jmx is expecting to run on a cluster where each machine has
    2 disk drives and there is one partition of data on each drive.
    For example the lineitem data would be located at:
      /mnt/disk1/data1/lineitem.tbl
      /mnt/disk2/data1/lineitem.tbl

(2) tpch-sf100-2D2F.jmx is expecting to run on a cluster where each machine has
    2 disk drives and there are two partitions of data on each drive.
    For example the lineitem data would be located at:
      /mnt/disk1/data1/lineitem.tbl
      /mnt/disk1/data2/lineitem.tbl
      /mnt/disk2/data1/lineitem.tbl
      /mnt/disk2/data2/lineitem.tbl

This is so that it can take advantage of parallel loading from multiple disks. But we can
also have the TPC-H data arranged in multiple files per disk in order to play with
the load concurrency.